# GitHub Copilot CLI — token example (Termux)

Copilot CLI reads a GitHub token from the environment, in this precedence
order (highest first):

    COPILOT_GITHUB_TOKEN  >  GH_TOKEN  >  GITHUB_TOKEN

If none is set it falls back to the system keychain and finally to
`gh auth token`. Termux has **no D-Bus Secret Service / gnome-keyring**, so the
keychain path does not work — prefer an environment variable.

## Recommended: a fine-grained personal access token

Create one at <https://github.com/settings/personal-access-tokens/new>:

- **Resource owner:** your **personal account** (organisation-owned tokens are
  not supported here).
- **Account permissions → Copilot Requests:** Allow.
- Classic tokens (`ghp_…`) are **rejected**.

An active **Copilot subscription/seat** is required regardless of the token.

Store it with restrictive permissions and export it from your shell profile:

    mkdir -p "$HOME/.config/copilot"
    printf '%s' 'github_pat_XXXXXXXX' > "$HOME/.config/copilot/token"
    chmod 600 "$HOME/.config/copilot/token"

    # add to ~/.bashrc
    [ -r "$HOME/.config/copilot/token" ] && \
        export COPILOT_GITHUB_TOKEN="$(cat "$HOME/.config/copilot/token")"

## Alternative: device-code login (no local browser needed)

    copilot login --device-code

Then open <https://github.com/login/device> (in any browser) and enter the
shown code. The CLI will warn that no system keychain is available; you can
allow plaintext storage in `~/.copilot/config.json` if you accept that tradeoff.
